<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pakistan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pakistan is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley and the north-western frontier of the subcontinent. In antiquity much of its territory belonged to successive empires. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> brought the region, along with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pakistan.jpg" alt="Pakistan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>India, <b>Pakistan</b>, Bangladesh and Myanmar inherited its borders, provinces, armies, railways, legal codes and civil services; the Indian Administrative Service and the Indian Penal Code descend directly from colonial institutions, and English became a language of government, law and higher education across the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At its peak it ruled present-day Afghanistan, much of <b>Pakistan</b>, parts of Iran, eastern Turkmenistan and north-western India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The name <b>Pakistan</b> was coined in January 1933 by the activist Choudhry Rahmat Ali in a pamphlet, Now or Never, as an acronym of Panjab, Afghania, Kashmir, Sindh and Baluchistan; in Persian and Urdu it also means 'the land of the pure'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In a war and treaty with Seleucus I Nicator around 305–303 BCE, he gained Arachosia, Gedrosia and the Paropamisadae, covering much of modern <b>Pakistan</b> and parts of Afghanistan, in return for 500 war elephants, and received the Greek envoy Megasthenes at his court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Independence came on 15 August 1947 together with partition: the Muslim-majority provinces of the north-west and east became <b>Pakistan</b>, and the division was accompanied by communal violence that killed hundreds of thousands and displaced some 15 million people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A mutual defence pact with <b>Pakistan</b> followed in September 2025, and the kingdom has said it would seek nuclear weapons if Iran acquires them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
